---
title: "Arena Allocation: Free a Thousand Objects in One Step"
author: celaltokmak61
date: 2026-11-28
abstract: A runnable guide to stdlib/arena.ltl — region-based allocation, slot ids, block rollover, O(1) reset, and the pinned vectors that keep the behavior honest.
---

## The pitch

When a job creates thousands of short-lived objects, freeing them one by one is wasted work. An arena allocates into blocks and frees the whole region at once. `stdlib/arena.ltl` gives you that model with a map-backed arena and integer slot ids.

```lateralus
import stdlib.arena

let a = stdlib.arena.new(2)
let r1 = stdlib.arena.alloc(a, "first")
a = r1[0]
let id = r1[1]
stdlib.arena.get(a, id)
```

## Construction

`new(capacity)` creates one empty block. `default()` is `new(1024)`. The arena tracks `blocks`, `current`, `capacity`, and `total`.

Pinned vector:

```lateralus
@test
fn default_uses_1024_capacity() -> bool {
    let a = stdlib.arena.default()
    return stdlib.arena.block_count(a) == 1 && stdlib.arena.total_capacity(a) == 1024
}
```

## Allocation and slot ids

`alloc` returns `[arena, id]`. The id is `block_index * capacity + slot_index`, so it is stable for the lifetime of the arena. When a block fills, the next allocation opens a new block.

Pinned vector:

```lateralus
@test
fn alloc_rolls_over_to_new_block() -> bool {
    let a = stdlib.arena.new(2)
    let r1 = stdlib.arena.alloc(a, 10); a = r1[0]
    let r2 = stdlib.arena.alloc(a, 20); a = r2[0]
    let r3 = stdlib.arena.alloc(a, 30); a = r3[0]
    return r1[1] == 0 && r2[1] == 1 && r3[1] == 2 &&
           stdlib.arena.block_count(a) == 2 &&
           stdlib.arena.get(a, 2) == 30
}
```

## Bulk allocation

`alloc_many` threads the arena through a list of values and returns `[arena, ids]`. It is the same loop you would write by hand.

Pinned vector:

```lateralus
@test
fn alloc_many_returns_ids_in_order() -> bool {
    let a = stdlib.arena.new(4)
    let result = stdlib.arena.alloc_many(a, [7, 8, 9])
    a = result[0]
    return result[1] == [0, 1, 2] && stdlib.arena.total(a) == 3
}
```

## Reads, writes, and bounds

`get` returns `nil` for an out-of-range id instead of throwing. `set` writes a slot in place and returns the updated arena.

Pinned vector:

```lateralus
@test
fn set_updates_and_get_rejects_unknown_slots() -> bool {
    let a = stdlib.arena.new(2)
    let r = stdlib.arena.alloc(a, 1); a = r[0]
    a = stdlib.arena.set(a, 0, 99)
    return stdlib.arena.get(a, 0) == 99 && stdlib.arena.get(a, 99) == nil
}
```

## Lifecycle and statistics

`reset` clears all blocks in O(1). `to_list` collects live values in allocation order. `stats` returns a map with total allocations, block count, capacity, remaining space, and usage ratio.

Pinned vectors:

```lateralus
@test
fn reset_clears_all_allocations() -> bool {
    let a = stdlib.arena.new(2)
    let r = stdlib.arena.alloc_many(a, [1, 2, 3]); a = r[0]
    a = stdlib.arena.reset(a)
    return stdlib.arena.total(a) == 0 && stdlib.arena.block_count(a) == 1 && stdlib.arena.to_list(a) == []
}

@test
fn stats_reports_capacity_and_usage() -> bool {
    let a = stdlib.arena.new(2)
    let r = stdlib.arena.alloc(a, 5); a = r[0]
    let s = stdlib.arena.stats(a)
    return s["total_allocations"] == 1 && s["block_count"] == 1 &&
           s["total_capacity"] == 2 && s["remaining_in_block"] == 1
}
```

## Runnable example

Save as `examples/arena_tutorial.ltl` and run `lateralus test examples/arena_tutorial.ltl`.

```lateralus
import stdlib.arena

@test
fn default_uses_1024_capacity() -> bool {
    let a = stdlib.arena.default()
    return stdlib.arena.block_count(a) == 1 && stdlib.arena.total_capacity(a) == 1024
}

@test
fn alloc_rolls_over_to_new_block() -> bool {
    let a = stdlib.arena.new(2)
    let r1 = stdlib.arena.alloc(a, 10); a = r1[0]
    let r2 = stdlib.arena.alloc(a, 20); a = r2[0]
    let r3 = stdlib.arena.alloc(a, 30); a = r3[0]
    return r1[1] == 0 && r2[1] == 1 && r3[1] == 2 &&
           stdlib.arena.block_count(a) == 2 && stdlib.arena.get(a, 2) == 30
}

@test
fn alloc_many_returns_ids_in_order() -> bool {
    let a = stdlib.arena.new(4)
    let result = stdlib.arena.alloc_many(a, [7, 8, 9])
    a = result[0]
    return result[1] == [0, 1, 2] && stdlib.arena.total(a) == 3
}

@test
fn set_updates_and_get_rejects_unknown_slots() -> bool {
    let a = stdlib.arena.new(2)
    let r = stdlib.arena.alloc(a, 1); a = r[0]
    a = stdlib.arena.set(a, 0, 99)
    return stdlib.arena.get(a, 0) == 99 && stdlib.arena.get(a, 99) == nil
}

@test
fn reset_clears_all_allocations() -> bool {
    let a = stdlib.arena.new(2)
    let r = stdlib.arena.alloc_many(a, [1, 2, 3]); a = r[0]
    a = stdlib.arena.reset(a)
    return stdlib.arena.total(a) == 0 && stdlib.arena.block_count(a) == 1 && stdlib.arena.to_list(a) == []
}

@test
fn stats_reports_capacity_and_usage() -> bool {
    let a = stdlib.arena.new(2)
    let r = stdlib.arena.alloc(a, 5); a = r[0]
    let s = stdlib.arena.stats(a)
    return s["total_allocations"] == 1 && s["block_count"] == 1 &&
           s["total_capacity"] == 2 && s["remaining_in_block"] == 1
}
```

Expected output:

```
6/6 tests passed
```

## When to use an arena

Arenas shine for parsers, per-request scratch space, graph traversals, and compiler passes: allocate freely, then drop the whole region. They are a poor fit when individual objects have very different lifetimes.
